--TEST--
Core\Json::encode writes a solidus as the byte it is and indents a pretty document by two spaces, where json_encode escapes every solidus unless told not to and indents by four with no spelling for anything else
--ORACLE-DIVERGES--
Two of `json_encode`'s fifteen `JSON_*` flags have no Novis spelling because their *other* setting is the only one spec § 6 offers. `json_encode` escapes every `/` as `\/` unless `JSON_UNESCAPED_SLASHES` is passed — a habit from embedding JSON in a `<script>` element, where the sequence that matters is `</`, not `/` — so PHP's default writes `"https:\/\/example.com\/a"` for a URL where `Core\Json::encode` writes it bare, and there is no option that turns Novis's writer into PHP's. And `JSON_PRETTY_PRINT` indents by four spaces where `{pretty: true}` indents by two, because the layout is `serde_json`'s `to_string_pretty` and Novis writes no JSON grammar of its own (`json.rs:20`); the two agree on everything else about the layout — the `": "` after a key, one element per line, and the empty array and empty object written on one line — so the whole divergence is the width. Both documents are the same JSON: a `/` and a `\/` are the same character to every reader, and indentation is insignificant.
--FILE--
<?nvs
// What is asked here is only the two flags whose sense is fixed. The escaping
// that *is* an option is `json-encode-escaping-unicode-matches-json_encodes-default-including-the-surrogate-pair`'s,
// and the rest of the writer is
// `json-encode-matches-json_encode-over-the-scalars-the-escapes-and-the-two-array-shapes`'s,
// whose oracle passes `JSON_UNESCAPED_SLASHES` precisely so that this row is
// asked once, here.

// A solidus is an ordinary character, in a value and in a key alike, and the
// sequence PHP's habit is actually about — `</` — gets no special treatment
// either. HTML is a sink with its own escaping (ADR 0024 § 3), and a document
// that has to survive a `<script>` element is escaped by that sink rather than
// by the JSON writer having a second mode.
echo Core\Json::encode("https://example.com/a?b=c"), "\n";
echo Core\Json::encode("</script>"), "\n";
echo Core\Json::encode(["a/b" => "c/d"]), "\n";

// A backslash before a solidus is still one escape, not two: `\/` in the
// output would be ambiguous about which of the two bytes was in the string.
echo Core\Json::encode("a\\/b"), "\n";

// Two spaces per level, at every depth, for both bracket shapes.
echo Core\Json::encode(["a" => 1, "b" => [1, 2]], {pretty: true}), "\n";
echo Core\Json::encode([["x" => [true]]], {pretty: true}), "\n";

// An empty array and an empty object are written on one line, which is the one
// place `JSON_PRETTY_PRINT` and this agree byte for byte.
array<int> $empty = [];
echo Core\Json::encode($empty, {pretty: true}), "\n";
echo Core\Json::encode(["none" => $empty], {pretty: true}), "\n";

// A scalar document has no layout to choose, so `pretty` is a no-op on one —
// which is also the shape the two options are asked together in, over in
// `json-encode-escaping-unicode-matches-json_encodes-default-including-the-surrogate-pair`,
// where a scalar is the one subject whose pretty spelling PHP agrees with.
echo Core\Json::encode(42, {pretty: true}), "\n";
--EXPECT--
"https://example.com/a?b=c"
"</script>"
{"a/b":"c/d"}
"a\\/b"
{
  "a": 1,
  "b": [
    1,
    2
  ]
}
[
  {
    "x": [
      true
    ]
  }
]
[]
{
  "none": []
}
42
